cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## dis.Instruction fields in 3.12 ONLY

  opcode        int
  opname        str
  arg           int|None
  argval        Any
  argrepr       str
  offset        int
  starts_line   int|None    ← LINE NUMBER if this instruction starts a new source line; else None
                              (This is the OLD semantics, DIFFERENT from 3.13+ bool)
  is_jump_target bool
  positions     dis.Positions  (added 3.11)

  MISSING in 3.12 (added later in 3.13):
    baseopcode, baseopname, oparg, start_offset, cache_offset, end_offset,
    line_number, jump_target, cache_info

  ## dis.Bytecode in 3.12
  Constructor:
    dis.Bytecode(x, *, first_line=None, current_offset=None,
                 show_caches=False, adaptive=False)
  No show_offsets, no show_positions.

  ## dis.get_instructions() in 3.12
  show_caches=True produces separate Instruction entries for CACHE pseudo-instructions.
  Each CACHE instruction has opname='CACHE'.

  ## Opcode collections in 3.12
  dis.hasarg    — added in 3.12
  dis.hasexc    — added in 3.12
  dis.hasjrel   — still used (not yet deprecated)
  dis.hasjabs   — still used (not yet deprecated/empty)

  ## Jump argument semantics in 3.12
  "The jump, exception handling, and loop instruction arguments are now instruction offset
   rather than byte offset." (Changed in 3.10)
  "The arg of jump instructions is now the instruction offset relative to the instruction
   following the CACHE entries, rather than to the start of the instruction."
  (Changed 3.12 — so in 3.12 jump arg is relative to after-cache, transparent for forward jumps)

  ## Key version change note (3.12 doc)
  Changed in 3.12: Jump argument is now target instruction offset RELATIVE to instruction after CACHE.
  Previously (3.11): relative to start of CACHE block.

  ## CACHE behavior in 3.12
  show_caches=True in get_instructions() → separate CACHE Instruction items emitted.
  CACHE instructions have opname='CACHE', they count toward bytecode offsets.
  When iterating to find block leaders: if show_caches=False (default), CACHE not in list.
  Offsets in returned instructions are byte offsets (each instruction word = 2 bytes).

  ## findlinestarts / findlabels
  dis.findlinestarts(code) — yields (offset, lineno) pairs for all line starts
  dis.findlabels(code)     — returns list of offsets that are jump targets (block leaders)
  Both available in 3.12.
<<<END>>>

