relevance: HIGH
title: dis.py source — Instruction class, _parse_exception_table, get_instructions implementation
authors: Python core developers
year: 2026
venue: github.com/python/cpython/blob/main/Lib/dis.py
quality: CODE
url: https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/main/Lib/dis.py
keywords: _parse_exception_table, _ExceptionTableEntry, _parse_varint, Instruction fields, get_instructions, Bytecode class
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## Instruction class definition (from dis.py main branch)
  _Instruction namedtuple extended with:
    Fields: opname, opcode, arg, argval, argrepr, offset, start_offset,
            starts_line, line_number, label, positions, cache_info
  Properties: oparg (alias for arg), baseopcode, baseopname, cache_offset, end_offset,
              jump_target, is_jump_target

  NOTE: The 'label' field appears in the internal namedtuple definition.
  This is the logical label used for display (e.g., "L1") not the byte offset.

  ## _parse_exception_table(code) — exact implementation

  def _parse_exception_table(code):
      iterator = iter(code.co_exceptiontable)
      entries = []
      try:
          while True:
              start = _parse_varint(iterator)*2
              length = _parse_varint(iterator)*2
              end = start + length
              target = _parse_varint(iterator)*2
              dl = _parse_varint(iterator)
              depth = dl >> 1
              lasti = bool(dl & 1)
              entries.append(_ExceptionTableEntry(start, end, target, depth, lasti))
      except StopIteration:
          return entries

  Return type: list of _ExceptionTableEntry namedtuples
  _ExceptionTableEntry fields: start, end, target, depth, lasti

  Semantics:
    start  — byte offset of first instruction covered by this entry
    end    — byte offset ONE PAST last instruction covered (exclusive)
    target — byte offset of the exception handler
    depth  — stack depth at start of try block (how many values to pop)
    lasti  — bool; if True, push the offset of the failing instruction onto stack before jumping to handler

  NOTE: *2 multiplication because co_exceptiontable stores instruction counts, not byte offsets.
        Each instruction = 2 bytes (word). So multiply by 2 to get byte offsets.

  ## _parse_varint(iterator) — varint decoding

  Reads variable-length integer from iterator over co_exceptiontable bytes.
  Format: 7-bit variant where each byte is SXdddddd:
    S = start-of-entry marker (MSB) — set for first byte of each field
    X = extend bit — if set, next byte continues this value
    dddddd = 6 data bits per byte

  ## Exception table entry interpretation for CFG edges

  Entry (start=20, end=28, target=100, depth=3, lasti=False) means:
    - Any instruction with byte offset in range [20, 28) (exclusive end)
      can raise an exception that transfers control to byte offset 100
    - At handler entry, stack is popped to depth=3, then exception pushed
    - lasti=False means: do NOT push instruction offset before jumping

  ## Jump target computation (from _get_jump_target() in dis.py)

  For relative jumps:
    target = offset + 2 + cache_size + arg * 2
    (offset = instruction start, +2 = size of instruction word, cache_size = CACHE entries after)

  For non-jump instructions: returns None

  ## _unpack_opargs() — bytecode unpacking
  Iterates over code.co_code (bytes), unpacking 2-byte (opcode, arg) words.
  Handles EXTENDED_ARG: accumulates arg across multiple EXTENDED_ARG prefixes.

  ## Bytecode class iteration
  Yields Instruction objects; each Instruction represents one logical instruction.
  In 3.13+: cache_info populated from CACHE entries that follow the instruction;
            no separate CACHE Instruction items emitted.
  In 3.12: if show_caches=True, separate CACHE Instruction items emitted after each
            instruction that has caches.
<<<END>>>

