id: 4
ts: 2026-05-28T00:04:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: CPython InternalDocs/exception_handling.md — Exception Table Binary Format
authors: Python core developers
year: 2025
venue: github.com/python/cpython/blob/main/InternalDocs/exception_handling.md
quality: DOCS
url: https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/main/InternalDocs/exception_handling.md
keywords: co_exceptiontable, exception table format, varint encoding, assemble_exception_table, get_exception_handler, depth lasti
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## Binary format of co_exceptiontable

  The exception table maps instruction offset ranges to their handler targets.
  Each entry encodes: (start-offset, size, target, depth, lasti)
    where:
      start-offset = byte offset of first instruction in protected range
      size         = (end - start), i.e. number of bytes covered (end is exclusive)
      target       = byte offset of exception handler
      depth        = stack depth at start of try block
      lasti        = 1 bit bool; if True, push the failing instruction's offset before jumping

  ## Varint encoding per field

  Each byte of the encoding has format: SXdddddd
    S (bit 7) = start-of-entry marker; set for first byte of each logical field
    X (bit 6) = extend bit; if set, next byte continues the same value
    dddddd (bits 5-0) = 6 data bits

  Fields are stored in sequence: start, size, target, (depth<<1)|lasti
    depth and lasti combined into single varint: value = (depth << 1) | lasti

  Stored in instruction counts (not bytes); multiply by 2 for byte offsets.

  ## Example encoding

  Entry (start=20, end=28, target=100, depth=3, lasti=False):
    start=20, size=8, target=100, depth_lasti=(3<<1)|0=6
    Binary: 148, 8, 65, 36, 6   (five bytes)

  ## Implementation references

  Construction: assemble_exception_table() in Python/assemble.c
  Runtime lookup: get_exception_handler() in Python/ceval.c
  Python parsing: _parse_exception_table() in Lib/dis.py

  ## RERAISE and lasti

  At the end of a finally block, when any in-flight exception needs to be propagated,
  RERAISE uses the lasti bit: if lasti=True was set for that entry, the original
  instruction pointer was pushed before entering the handler, and RERAISE uses it
  to restore the "last instruction" before re-raising.
  This allows accurate traceback information during finally-block propagation.

  ## CFG edge semantics

  For CFG construction:
    Exception table entry (start, end, target, depth, lasti) creates an exception edge
    from every instruction I where start <= I.offset < end, to the basic block
    starting at offset=target.
    Multiple instructions can share the same exception handler target.
    Entries can overlap or be nested (inner try within outer try).
<<<END>>>


