id: 5
ts: 2026-05-28T00:05:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: dis.py source — Instruction class, _parse_exception_table, _parse_varint, _get_jump_target, Bytecode (raw GitHub)
authors: Python core developers
year: 2026
venue: raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Lib/dis.py
quality: CODE
url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Lib/dis.py
keywords: Instruction namedtuple fields, _parse_exception_table, _parse_varint, _get_jump_target, _unpack_opargs, findlabels, findlinestarts, Bytecode class, _make_labels_map
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## Instruction namedtuple definition (verbatim)

  _Instruction = collections.namedtuple(
      "_Instruction",
      [
          'opname', 'opcode', 'arg', 'argval', 'argrepr',
          'offset', 'start_offset', 'starts_line', 'line_number',
          'label', 'positions', 'cache_info',
      ],
      defaults=[None, None, None]
  )

  IMPORTANT: 'label' is a field in the namedtuple (the logical label for display, e.g. "L1").
  'is_jump_target' is now a PROPERTY: return self.label is not None
  'jump_target' is a PROPERTY: calls _get_jump_target(self.opcode, self.arg, self.offset)
  'oparg' is a PROPERTY: alias for self.arg
  'baseopcode' is a PROPERTY: _deoptop(self.opcode)
  'baseopname' is a PROPERTY: opname[self.baseopcode]
  'cache_offset' is a PROPERTY: self.offset + 2
  'end_offset' is a PROPERTY: self.cache_offset + _get_cache_size(_all_opname[self.opcode])*2

  Instruction.make() static method:
    def make(opname, arg, argval, argrepr, offset, start_offset, starts_line,
             line_number, label=None, positions=None, cache_info=None):
        return Instruction(opname, _all_opmap[opname], arg, argval, argrepr, offset,
                           start_offset, starts_line, line_number, label, positions, cache_info)

  ## _parse_varint (verbatim)

  def _parse_varint(iterator):
      b = next(iterator)
      val = b & 63          # bits 0-5 of first byte
      while b & 64:         # bit 6 = extend bit
          val <<= 6
          b = next(iterator)
          val |= b & 63
      return val

  NOTE: This differs slightly from the inline-cache varint described in InternalDocs.
  Here bits 0-5 are data (6 bits), bit 6 is extend. bit 7 (S=start-of-entry) is not checked
  by _parse_varint — entries are already demarcated by sequential parsing order.

  ## _parse_exception_table (verbatim)

  def _parse_exception_table(code):
      iterator = iter(code.co_exceptiontable)
      entries = []
      try:
          while True:
              start = _parse_varint(iterator)*2
              length = _parse_varint(iterator)*2
              end = start + length
              target = _parse_varint(iterator)*2
              dl = _parse_varint(iterator)
              depth = dl >> 1
              lasti = bool(dl & 1)
              entries.append(_ExceptionTableEntry(start, end, target, depth, lasti))
      except StopIteration:
          return entries

  Return type: list[_ExceptionTableEntry]
  _ExceptionTableEntry = namedtuple('_ExceptionTableEntry', 'start end target depth lasti')
  Fields:
    start  — byte offset, INCLUSIVE lower bound of protected range
    end    — byte offset, EXCLUSIVE upper bound (start + length*2)
    target — byte offset of exception handler block leader
    depth  — stack depth at entry to try block
    lasti  — bool: if True, push the failing instruction's offset before handler entry

  ## _get_jump_target (verbatim)

  def _get_jump_target(op, arg, offset):
      deop = _deoptop(op)
      caches = _get_cache_size(_all_opname[deop])
      if deop in hasjrel:
          if _is_backward_jump(deop):
              arg = -arg
          target = offset + 2 + arg*2
          target += 2 * caches
      elif deop in hasjabs:
          target = arg*2
      else:
          target = None
      return target

  NOTE: In 3.13, hasjabs is empty (all jumps are relative). Only hasjrel matters.
  For backward jumps (JUMP_BACKWARD etc.), arg is negated.
  Forward jump formula: offset + 2 + arg*2 + 2*cache_size
    (offset+2 = past this instruction, arg*2 = instruction count to byte offset,
     2*cache_size = skip over CACHE entries)

  ## _unpack_opargs (verbatim)

  def _unpack_opargs(code):
      extended_arg = 0
      extended_args_offset = 0
      caches = 0
      for i in range(0, len(code), 2):
          if caches:
              caches -= 1
              continue
          op = code[i]
          deop = _deoptop(op)
          caches = _get_cache_size(_all_opname[deop])
          if deop in hasarg:
              arg = code[i+1] | extended_arg
              extended_arg = (arg << 8) if deop == EXTENDED_ARG else 0
              if extended_arg >= _INT_OVERFLOW:
                  extended_arg -= 2 * _INT_OVERFLOW
          else:
              arg = None
              extended_arg = 0
          if deop == EXTENDED_ARG:
              extended_args_offset += 1
              yield (i, i, op, arg)
          else:
              start_offset = i - extended_args_offset*2
              yield (i, start_offset, op, arg)
              extended_args_offset = 0

  Yields: (offset, start_offset, op, arg)
    offset — byte offset of THIS instruction word
    start_offset — byte offset of first EXTENDED_ARG prefix (or same as offset if none)
    op — raw opcode byte
    arg — accumulated arg including EXTENDED_ARG prefixes

  ## findlabels (verbatim)

  def findlabels(code):
      labels = []
      for offset, _, op, arg in _unpack_opargs(code):
          if arg is not None:
              label = _get_jump_target(op, arg, offset)
              if label is None:
                  continue
              if label not in labels:
                  labels.append(label)
      return labels

  Returns list of byte offsets that are jump targets (block leaders).
  Also used internally to build labels_map for the Bytecode display.

  ## findlinestarts (verbatim)

  def findlinestarts(code):
      lastline = False
      for start, end, line in code.co_lines():
          if line is not lastline:
              lastline = line
              yield start, line
      return

  Yields: (offset, lineno) pairs where a new source line starts.
  Uses co_lines() method (available since 3.10).

  ## Bytecode.__init__ (verbatim key parts)

  self.codeobj = co = _get_code_object(x)
  self._linestarts = dict(findlinestarts(co))
  self.exception_entries = _parse_exception_table(co)
  ...

  Key: exception_entries is populated on Bytecode construction.
  Can access as bytecode_obj.exception_entries for CFG edge building.

  ## Bytecode.__iter__ (key parts)

  labels_map = _make_labels_map(original_code, self.exception_entries)
  arg_resolver = ArgResolver(co_consts=co.co_consts, ...)
  return _get_instructions_bytes(..., arg_resolver=arg_resolver)

  Labels map uses BOTH jump targets (from _get_jump_target) AND exception handler targets
  (from exception_entries) to assign logical labels.

  ## _get_instructions_bytes (key parts)

  starts_line computed from linestarts dict:
    starts_line = offset in linestarts
  line_number from linestarts[offset] if starts_line else carries forward local_line_number.

  cache_info built by reading CACHE bytes from code array:
    cache_info = [(name, size, data), ...] where data is raw bytes.

  label = arg_resolver.get_label_for_offset(offset)
  is_jump_target derived from: self.label is not None
<<<END>>>

