id: 6
ts: 2026-05-28T00:06:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Python 3.14 opcode changes — RETURN_CONST removed, BEFORE_WITH removed, new opcodes
authors: Python core developers
year: 2024-2025
venue: docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/3.14.html
quality: DOCS
url: https://docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/3.14.html
keywords: RETURN_CONST removed, BEFORE_WITH removed, BEFORE_ASYNC_WITH removed, LOAD_SMALL_INT, LOAD_FAST_BORROW, LOAD_SPECIAL, POP_ITER, NOT_TAKEN, BUILD_TEMPLATE, BUILD_INTERPOLATION
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## Python 3.14 opcode additions and removals

  REMOVED in 3.14:
    RETURN_CONST   — existed in 3.12 and 3.13; removed in 3.14 (gh-125837, Mark Shannon)
                     Was: return a constant directly from co_consts without loading.
                     Now: RETURN_VALUE is used (with LOAD_CONST or LOAD_SMALL_INT preceding it).
    BEFORE_WITH         — removed in 3.14
    BEFORE_ASYNC_WITH   — removed in 3.14
    (with/async-with now uses LOAD_SPECIAL instead)

  ADDED in 3.14:
    LOAD_SMALL_INT    — pushes a small integer equal to oparg to the stack
    LOAD_FAST_BORROW  — reduced reference-counting overhead for local vars
    LOAD_FAST_BORROW_LOAD_FAST_BORROW — optimized variant
    LOAD_SPECIAL      — new instruction for with/async-with statements
    POP_ITER          — pops loop iterator
    NOT_TAKEN         — (branch prediction hint?)
    LOAD_COMMON_CONSTANT — loads commonly-used constants
    BUILD_TEMPLATE    — for f-string template construction
    BUILD_INTERPOLATION — for f-string interpolation

  ## CRITICAL for pflow CFG construction

  RETURN_CONST status:
    3.12: EXISTS — RETURN_CONST terminates a basic block (like RETURN_VALUE)
    3.13: EXISTS — RETURN_CONST terminates a basic block
    3.14: REMOVED — only RETURN_VALUE terminates via return

  BEFORE_WITH status:
    3.12: EXISTS — used for with-statement __enter__ call setup
    3.13: EXISTS
    3.14: REMOVED — with-statement now uses LOAD_SPECIAL

  This means CFG terminator opcode lists MUST be version-gated.

  ## Basic block terminators by version (compiled from multiple sources)

  All versions (3.12, 3.13, 3.14):
    RETURN_VALUE      — function return (always terminates)
    RAISE_VARARGS     — raise with 0/1/2 args (always terminates)
    RERAISE           — reraise exception (always terminates; oparg>0 pops lasti)
    JUMP_FORWARD      — unconditional forward jump
    JUMP_BACKWARD     — unconditional backward jump (loop back-edge)
    POP_JUMP_IF_TRUE  — conditional jump
    POP_JUMP_IF_FALSE — conditional jump
    POP_JUMP_IF_NONE  — conditional jump
    POP_JUMP_IF_NOT_NONE — conditional jump

  3.12 and 3.13 only:
    RETURN_CONST      — terminates block (removed in 3.14)

  3.12 and 3.13:
    BEFORE_WITH       — NOT a terminator; calls __enter__

  3.14:
    LOAD_SPECIAL      — NOT a terminator (replaces BEFORE_WITH but doesn't end block)

  ## Jump instruction argument semantics (all 3.12-3.14)
  All jumps are relative to instruction after CACHE entries.
  No absolute jumps (hasjabs is empty since 3.13; was depopulated in 3.12 mostly).

  ## RERAISE opcode details
  RERAISE oparg=0: reraise exception from TOS (no lasti use)
  RERAISE oparg=1: pop lasti from stack, set f_lasti, then reraise
  Used at end of finally blocks when exception needs to propagate.

  ## RAISE_VARARGS details
  oparg=0: re-raise (must be inside except block)
  oparg=1: raise exc (pops 1)
  oparg=2: raise exc from cause (pops 2)
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 7
ts: 2026-05-28T00:07:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Python/flowgraph.c — CPython internal CFG representation and construction
authors: Python core developers
year: 2026
venue: github.com/python/cpython/blob/main/Python/flowgraph.c
quality: CODE
url: https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/main/Python/flowgraph.c
keywords: basicblock struct, IS_SCOPE_EXIT_OPCODE, IS_UNCONDITIONAL_JUMP_OPCODE, basicblock_nofallthrough, label_exception_targets, _PyCfgExceptStack, b_preserve_lasti, b_except_handler
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## basicblock struct definition (verbatim from flowgraph.c)

  typedef struct _PyCfgBasicblock {
      struct _PyCfgBasicblock *b_list;       /* reverse linked list of blocks */
      _PyJumpTargetLabel b_label;             /* jump target label, -1 if none */
      struct _PyCfgExceptStack *b_exceptstack; /* exception stack at entry */
      cfg_instr *b_instr;                    /* instruction array pointer */
      struct _PyCfgBasicblock *b_next;       /* next block by control flow */
      int b_iused;                           /* number of instructions used */
      int b_ialloc;                          /* allocated instruction array size */
      uint64_t b_unsafe_locals_mask;
      int b_predecessors;                    /* predecessor block count */
      int b_startdepth;                      /* stack depth upon entry */
      unsigned b_preserve_lasti : 1;         /* exception handler preserves lasti */
      unsigned b_visited : 1;                /* compiler pass visitor flag */
      unsigned b_except_handler : 1;         /* marks exception targets */
      unsigned b_cold : 1;                   /* non-perf-critical block flag */
      unsigned b_warm : 1;                   /* definitely not cold flag */
  } basicblock;

  ## _PyCfgExceptStack

  struct _PyCfgExceptStack {
      basicblock *handlers[CO_MAXBLOCKS+2];
      int depth;
  };

  ## Block termination detection

  static inline int
  basicblock_nofallthrough(const basicblock *b) {
      cfg_instr *last = basicblock_last_instr(b);
      return (last &&
              (IS_SCOPE_EXIT_OPCODE(last->i_opcode) ||
               IS_UNCONDITIONAL_JUMP_OPCODE(last->i_opcode)));
  }

  IS_SCOPE_EXIT_OPCODE — covers RETURN_VALUE, RAISE_VARARGS, RERAISE (and RETURN_CONST in 3.12/3.13)
  IS_UNCONDITIONAL_JUMP_OPCODE — covers JUMP_FORWARD, JUMP_BACKWARD, JUMP_BACKWARD_NO_INTERRUPT

  ## Public CFG API

  cfg_builder *_PyCfgBuilder_New(void)
  void _PyCfgBuilder_Free(cfg_builder *g)
  int _PyCfgBuilder_CheckSize(cfg_builder *g)
  int _PyCfgBuilder_Addop(cfg_builder *g, int opcode, int oparg, location loc)
  int _PyCfgBuilder_UseLabel(cfg_builder *g, jump_target_label lbl)

  ## Exception labeling

  label_exception_targets() — depth-first traversal; copies exception stack at block
    boundaries; marks each instruction's i_except field with active handler block.
  SETUP_WITH / SETUP_CLEANUP pseudo-instructions trigger b_preserve_lasti flagging.

  ## Finally block handling

  From search result data and CPython documentation:
  CPython DUPLICATES the finally clause body into the exception path AND the normal path.
  Each try/finally compiles to:
    1. The try body
    2. (normal exit path) finally body copy + continuation
    3. (exception path) finally body copy + RERAISE
  The exception table points to the exception-path copy of the finally body.
  This means a finally block may appear TWICE in the bytecode — once for normal flow,
  once for exception flow (reached via exception table).

  ## Instruction macros

  #define INSTR_SET_OP1(I, OP, ARG) ...
  #define INSTR_SET_OP0(I, OP) ...
  #define INSTR_SET_LOC(I, LOC) ...

  cfg_instr struct has fields: i_opcode, i_oparg, i_loc, i_except (pointer to handler block)

  ## Optimization pipeline

  optimize_cfg() orchestrates passes:
    - constant folding
    - jump threading
    - redundant instruction removal
    - SWAP optimization
    - remove_redundant_nops_and_jumps()

  Changes in optimization use NOP placeholders, later compacted.
<<<END>>>

