id: 9
ts: 2026-05-28T00:09:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Python 3.12 What's New — bytecode/opcode changes
authors: Python core developers
year: 2024
venue: docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/3.12.html
quality: DOCS
url: https://docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/3.12.html
keywords: RETURN_CONST added, LOAD_FAST_AND_CLEAR, LOAD_FAST_CHECK, LOAD_SUPER_ATTR, CLEANUP_THROW, hasarg, hasexc, POP_JUMP_IF_* no longer pseudo, jump argument semantics
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## Python 3.12 opcode additions

  ADDED in 3.12:
    RETURN_CONST(consti)     — return co_consts[consti]; terminates block
                               (gh-101632, Wenyang Wang)
    BINARY_SLICE             — replaces slice subscript operations (gh-94163, Mark Shannon)
    STORE_SLICE              — (gh-94163, Mark Shannon)
    CALL_INTRINSIC_1         — call intrinsic with one arg (gh-99005, Mark Shannon)
    CALL_INTRINSIC_2         — call intrinsic with two args (gh-101799, Irit Katriel)
    CLEANUP_THROW            — exception cleanup for throw()/close() on generators (gh-90997)
    END_SEND                 — generator send protocol (gh-103082)
    LOAD_FAST_AND_CLEAR      — for PEP 709 comprehension inlining (gh-101441)
    LOAD_FAST_CHECK          — check if local var initialized (gh-93143)
    LOAD_FROM_DICT_OR_DEREF  — PEP 695 type params (gh-103764)
    LOAD_FROM_DICT_OR_GLOBALS — PEP 695 type params (gh-103764)
    LOAD_LOCALS              — PEP 695 type params (gh-103764)
    LOAD_SUPER_ATTR          — optimized super() calls; 2x-5x speedup (gh-103497)

  REMOVED in 3.12:
    LOAD_METHOD      — merged into LOAD_ATTR; low bit of LOAD_ATTR oparg now signals method-call
    JUMP_IF_FALSE_OR_POP  — removed (gh-102859)
    JUMP_IF_TRUE_OR_POP   — removed (gh-102859)
    PRECALL          — removed (gh-92925)
    LOAD_CLASSDEREF  — replaced by LOAD_LOCALS + LOAD_FROM_DICT_OR_DEREF

  ## dis module changes in 3.12
    dis.hasarg  — added (gh-94216, Irit Katriel)
    dis.hasexc  — added (gh-94216, Irit Katriel)
    Pseudo instructions now exposed in collections

  ## Jump argument semantics change in 3.12
  "Jump argument is now the instruction offset relative to the instruction following
   the CACHE entries, rather than to the start of the instruction."
  (Was: relative to start of instruction in 3.11)

  ## POP_JUMP_IF_* change in 3.12
  POP_JUMP_IF_TRUE, POP_JUMP_IF_FALSE, POP_JUMP_IF_NOT_NONE, POP_JUMP_IF_NONE
  no longer pseudo-instructions in 3.12 (became real opcodes).

  ## COMPARE_OP change in 3.12
  cmp_op index now stored in four HIGHEST bits (not four lowest).

  ## LOAD_ATTR change in 3.12
  Low bit signals method-call behavior (merging old LOAD_METHOD semantics).
  oparg bit 0 = 0: load attribute (old LOAD_ATTR)
  oparg bit 0 = 1: load method (old LOAD_METHOD)

  ## BEFORE_WITH in 3.12
  BEFORE_WITH exists in 3.12; NOT yet removed.
  Used for with-statement __enter__ call setup.

  ## try/finally duplication note
  "CPython duplicates the code for a finally clause" — confirmed from python-dev mailing list.
  Both normal-exit and exception-exit paths contain copies of the finally body.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 10
ts: 2026-05-28T00:10:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Python Bytecode Instructions reference — control-flow-relevant opcodes (3.14 docs)
authors: Python core developers
year: 2026
venue: docs.python.org/3/library/dis.html#python-bytecode-instructions
quality: DOCS
url: https://docs.python.org/3/library/dis.html#python-bytecode-instructions
keywords: RETURN_VALUE, RERAISE, RAISE_VARARGS, PUSH_EXC_INFO, POP_EXCEPT, CHECK_EXC_MATCH, WITH_EXCEPT_START, CLEANUP_THROW, JUMP_FORWARD, JUMP_BACKWARD, POP_JUMP_IF_TRUE, FOR_ITER, SEND, LOAD_SPECIAL, POP_ITER
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## Return instructions

  RETURN_VALUE
    Returns STACK[-1] to caller. No oparg.
    Terminates basic block (scope exit).
    All versions 3.12-3.14.

  RETURN_CONST(consti)
    3.12-3.13 ONLY: Returns co_consts[consti] without loading.
    Terminates basic block.
    REMOVED in 3.14.

  RETURN_GENERATOR
    Creates generator/coroutine/async-generator from current frame; returns it.
    Used as first opcode in generator function code objects.
    Added 3.11.

  ## Exception instructions

  RAISE_VARARGS(argc)
    oparg=0: re-raise current exception
    oparg=1: raise STACK[-1]
    oparg=2: raise STACK[-2] from STACK[-1]
    Terminates basic block (unconditionally).

  RERAISE(lasti)
    Re-raises TOS exception.
    oparg non-zero: pop additional value from stack, use to set f_lasti of current frame.
    Added 3.9. Changed 3.11 (exception = 1 item, not 3).
    Terminates basic block (unconditionally).

  PUSH_EXC_INFO
    Pops value from stack.
    Pushes current exception to top.
    Pushes popped value back.
    Used in exception handlers.
    Added 3.11.
    NOT a terminator.

  POP_EXCEPT
    Pops exception state restoration value.
    Changed 3.11 (1 item, not 3).
    NOT a terminator.

  CHECK_EXC_MATCH
    Tests STACK[-2] isinstance STACK[-1]. Pops STACK[-1], pushes bool result.
    Added 3.11.
    NOT a terminator.

  CHECK_EG_MATCH
    Exception group matching for except*.
    Added 3.11.
    NOT a terminator.

  CLEANUP_THROW
    Handles exceptions during throw()/close() calls.
    If STACK[-1] is StopIteration: pop 3, push StopIteration.value.
    Else: reraise STACK[-1].
    Added 3.12.
    Conditionally terminates (acts like jump in exception case).

  WITH_EXCEPT_START
    Calls __exit__(type, val, tb) at stack position 4 for exceptions in with-body.
    Used when exception occurs in with-statement body.
    Added 3.9. Changed 3.11 (__exit__ now at position 4, not 7).
    NOT a terminator; result used by POP_JUMP_IF_TRUE to exit or RERAISE.
    In 3.12+: exception table entry covers the with-body, handler calls WITH_EXCEPT_START.

  ## Jump instructions

  JUMP_FORWARD(delta)
    Increment bytecode counter by delta.
    Unconditional. Terminates block.

  JUMP_BACKWARD(delta)
    Decrement bytecode counter by delta. Checks interrupts.
    Unconditional. Terminates block.
    Added 3.11.

  JUMP_BACKWARD_NO_INTERRUPT(delta)
    Backward jump without interrupt check.
    Unconditional. Terminates block.
    Added 3.11.

  POP_JUMP_IF_TRUE(delta)
    If STACK[-1] is true: increment counter by delta. Pop STACK[-1].
    Conditional. Two successors: jump target + fallthrough.
    Changed 3.11 (relative delta, was absolute; pseudo-instruction).
    Changed 3.12 (no longer pseudo).
    Changed 3.13 (requires exact bool operand).

  POP_JUMP_IF_FALSE(delta)
    If STACK[-1] is false: increment counter by delta. Pop STACK[-1].
    Conditional. Two successors.
    Same version history as POP_JUMP_IF_TRUE.

  POP_JUMP_IF_NOT_NONE(delta)
    If STACK[-1] is not None: jump. Pop.
    Conditional. Added 3.11. Changed 3.12 (no longer pseudo).

  POP_JUMP_IF_NONE(delta)
    If STACK[-1] is None: jump. Pop.
    Conditional. Added 3.11. Changed 3.12 (no longer pseudo).

  FOR_ITER(delta)
    Call STACK[-1].__next__(). Push new value, or jump delta if exhausted.
    Conditional terminator (two successors).
    Changed 3.12 (iterator no longer popped when exhausted until 3.12+).

  SEND(delta)
    STACK[-1] = STACK[-2].send(STACK[-1]). For yield-from/await.
    If StopIteration raised: pop TOS, push .value, jump delta.
    Conditional terminator.
    Added 3.11.

  ## Context manager / with-statement instructions

  BEFORE_WITH
    3.12 and 3.13 ONLY.
    Sets up with-statement: loads __exit__, calls __enter__, result left on stack.
    REMOVED in 3.14.
    NOT a basic-block terminator.

  LOAD_SPECIAL(namei)
    3.14+. Replaces BEFORE_WITH and BEFORE_ASYNC_WITH.
    Performs special method lookup on STACK[-1].
    oparg: encodes which special method (via dedicated enum).
    Not a basic-block terminator.

  ## Iteration

  POP_ITER
    3.14+. Removes iterator from TOS.
    Added 3.14.

  ## Key CFG construction note

  For CFG building from bytecode:
  Block leaders are:
    1. First instruction in code object
    2. Any instruction targeted by a jump (is_jump_target=True / label is not None)
    3. Any instruction targeted by an exception table entry (target field)
    4. Instruction immediately after an unconditional terminator (dead code / new block start)

  Exception edges: for each exception table entry (start, end, target):
    Every instruction with start <= offset < end has an exception edge to target.
    The instruction at target is a block leader; its block is an exception handler.
    b_except_handler = True for that block in CPython's internal representation.
<<<END>>>


