id: 11
ts: 2026-05-28T00:11:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pycore_opcode_metadata.h — opcode flag definitions and IS_PSEUDO_INSTR macro
authors: Python core developers (generated file)
year: 2026
venue: raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Include/internal/pycore_opcode_metadata.h
quality: CODE
url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Include/internal/pycore_opcode_metadata.h
keywords: HAS_ARG_FLAG, HAS_JUMP_FLAG, HAS_ERROR_FLAG, HAS_ESCAPES_FLAG, IS_PSEUDO_INSTR, opcode flags, SETUP_FINALLY, SETUP_CLEANUP, SETUP_WITH, POP_BLOCK
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## IS_PSEUDO_INSTR macro (verbatim)

  #define IS_PSEUDO_INSTR(OP)  ( \
      ((OP) == LOAD_CLOSURE) || \
      ((OP) == STORE_FAST_MAYBE_NULL) || \
      ((OP) == ANNOTATIONS_PLACEHOLDER) || \
      ((OP) == JUMP) || \
      ((OP) == JUMP_NO_INTERRUPT) || \
      ((OP) == JUMP_IF_FALSE) || \
      ((OP) == JUMP_IF_TRUE) || \
      ((OP) == SETUP_FINALLY) || \
      ((OP) == SETUP_CLEANUP) || \
      ((OP) == SETUP_WITH) || \
      ((OP) == POP_BLOCK) || \
      0)

  These are pseudo-instructions: emitted by codegen but never reach compiled bytecode.
  Replaced by exception table entries + real opcodes during assembly.

  ## Opcode flag definitions (verbatim)

  HAS_ARG_FLAG        = 1   — instruction has argument
  HAS_CONST_FLAG      = 2   — references constant table
  HAS_NAME_FLAG       = 4   — references name table
  HAS_JUMP_FLAG       = 8   — control flow instruction
  HAS_FREE_FLAG       = 16  — references free variables
  HAS_LOCAL_FLAG      = 32  — references local variables
  HAS_EVAL_BREAK_FLAG = 64  — allows evaluation break (check signals)
  HAS_ERROR_FLAG      = 256 — can raise exceptions
  HAS_ESCAPES_FLAG    = 512 — may transfer control out of current instruction sequence

  ## Metadata for key opcodes (from _PyOpcode_metadata table)

  RETURN_VALUE: format=INSTR_FMT_IX, flags=HAS_ESCAPES_FLAG|HAS_NEEDS_GUARD_IP_FLAG
    pops=1, pushes=1 (effectfully exits)
  RERAISE: format=INSTR_FMT_IB, flags=HAS_ARG_FLAG|HAS_ERROR_FLAG|HAS_ERROR_NO_POP_FLAG|HAS_ESCAPES_FLAG
    pops=1+oparg
  RAISE_VARARGS: format=INSTR_FMT_IB, flags=HAS_ARG_FLAG
    pops=oparg, pushes=0
  JUMP_FORWARD: format=INSTR_FMT_IB, flags=HAS_ARG_FLAG|HAS_JUMP_FLAG
    pops=0, pushes=0
  JUMP_BACKWARD: format=INSTR_FMT_IBC, flags=HAS_JUMP_FLAG|HAS_EVAL_BREAK_FLAG
    pops=0, pushes=0

  ## IS_TERMINATOR_OPCODE
  NOT explicitly defined as a macro in this header.
  Terminator identification is done through IS_SCOPE_EXIT_OPCODE + IS_UNCONDITIONAL_JUMP_OPCODE
  in flowgraph.c; these in turn reference opcode properties.
  From context: IS_SCOPE_EXIT_OPCODE covers opcodes with HAS_ESCAPES_FLAG that return/raise.

  ## Note on generated nature
  pycore_opcode_metadata.h is generated by Tools/cases_generator/generate_cases.py.
  Not manually edited. Regenerated whenever Python/bytecodes.c changes.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 12
ts: 2026-05-28T00:12:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: CPython codegen.c — try/finally and try/except compilation (compiler_try_finally, compiler_try_except)
authors: Python core developers
year: 2026
venue: raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Python/codegen.c
quality: CODE
url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Python/codegen.c
keywords: codegen_try_finally, codegen_try_except, SETUP_FINALLY, SETUP_CLEANUP, PUSH_EXC_INFO, RERAISE, POP_EXCEPT, CHECK_EXC_MATCH, POP_JUMP_IF_FALSE, codegen_with, BEFORE_WITH
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## Function declarations (from codegen.c)

  static int codegen_with(compiler *, stmt_ty);
  static int codegen_async_with(compiler *, stmt_ty);
  static int codegen_with_inner(compiler *, stmt_ty, int);
  static int codegen_async_with_inner(compiler *, stmt_ty, int);

  NOTE: Implementations of codegen_with / codegen_async_with not extractable from
  truncated fetch. Use information from dis output examples instead (see record 13).

  ## codegen_try_finally() — key opcode sequence

  Pseudo-instructions emitted (before assembly):
    SETUP_FINALLY  (pseudo: marks start of try block; becomes exception table entry)
    <body>
    POP_BLOCK      (pseudo: marks end of protected region)
    <finally_body> (normal exit copy)
    JUMP <after>
    SETUP_CLEANUP  (pseudo: marks exception handler entry)
    PUSH_EXC_INFO
    <finally_body> (exception path copy — DUPLICATED!)
    RERAISE        (re-raise after finally)

  Key: finally body IS DUPLICATED. One copy for normal exit, one for exception path.
  The duplication happens before the CFG optimization pass.

  ## codegen_try_except() — key opcode sequence

  SETUP_FINALLY  (pseudo: try block start)
  <body>
  POP_BLOCK      (pseudo: end of protected region)
  JUMP <after>
  SETUP_CLEANUP  (pseudo: exception handler)
  PUSH_EXC_INFO
  [for each except clause:]
    CHECK_EXC_MATCH
    POP_JUMP_IF_FALSE <next_handler>
    <handler_body>
    POP_EXCEPT
    JUMP <after>
  RERAISE        (if no handler matched)
  <after>:

  ## codegen_try_star_except() (for except*)

  Uses CHECK_EG_MATCH for exception group matching (PEP 654).
  CALL_INTRINSIC_2 with INTRINSIC_PREP_RERAISE_STAR.
  BUILD_LIST for collecting re-raised exceptions.

  ## Pseudo-instruction to real bytecode transformation

  SETUP_FINALLY → exception table entry: (start, end, target, depth, lasti)
  SETUP_CLEANUP → exception table entry
  SETUP_WITH    → exception table entry
  POP_BLOCK     → removed (no bytecode emitted)

  The transformation occurs during the assembly pass, NOT during codegen.
  At Python level, dis shows ONLY the real bytecode after transformation.

  ## compile.c vs codegen.c split (since 3.12-3.13 timeframe)

  compile.c was split into compile.c (compiler state management) + codegen.c (code generation).
  See gh-121404 for the split. Both files are in Python/ directory.
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 13
ts: 2026-05-28T00:13:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: co_lines(), co_positions(), PyCode_Addr2Location — source position mapping API
authors: Python core developers
year: 2026
venue: docs.python.org/3/c-api/code.html + peps.python.org/pep-0626/ + InternalDocs/code_objects.md
quality: DOCS
url: https://docs.python.org/3/c-api/code.html
keywords: co_lines, co_positions, co_linetable, PyCode_Addr2Line, PyCode_Addr2Location, PEP 626, source position, None positions
cites: PEP 626
cited_by:
content:
  ## co_lines() method (PEP 626, added 3.10)

  code.co_lines()
  Returns: iterator of (start, end, line) tuples
    start — byte offset (inclusive) of range start
    end   — byte offset (exclusive) of range end
    line  — int line number, or None for instructions without source line
  Properties:
    - First range starts at offset 0
    - Ranges are non-decreasing and consecutive
    - Final range ends at bytecode size
    - Zero-width ranges (start == end) permitted for eliminated source lines
  Semantics: gives line number of instructions in [start, end).
  If line is None: that instruction range has no associated source line.

  ## co_positions() method (added 3.11)

  code.co_positions()
  Returns: iterator of (line, end_line, column, end_column) tuples
    ONE TUPLE PER INSTRUCTION (including CACHE instructions if present)
    tuple values may be None if position information is not available
  Key: iterator advances one step per instruction INCLUDING cache entries.
  dis._get_instructions_bytes() calls: for _ in range(caches): next(co_positions, ())
    to skip cache-entry tuples when building Instruction objects.

  dis.Positions namedtuple fields map directly from co_positions() tuple:
    Positions(lineno, end_lineno, col_offset, end_col_offset)

  ## co_linetable encoding (internal format)

  Compact encoding in co_linetable bytes.
  Control byte has bit 7 set; optional continuation bytes follow.
  Entry types (code in bits 3-0 of control byte):
    0-9:  Short form — two bytes; start_col = (code*8) + ((second_byte>>4)&7)
    10-12: One-line form — single-line entries with unsigned byte columns
    13:   No column info — omits column data
    14:   Long form — full 4-tuple with varint encoding
    15:   No location — placeholder (maps to None in co_positions)

  ## PyCode_Addr2Line (C API)

  int PyCode_Addr2Line(PyCodeObject *co, int byte_offset)
  Returns: line number of instruction at or before byte_offset
  For frame use, prefer PyFrame_GetLineNumber().
  For iteration, prefer PEP 626 co_lines().

  ## PyCode_Addr2Location (C API, added 3.11)

  int PyCode_Addr2Location(PyObject *co, int byte_offset,
                            int *start_line, int *start_column,
                            int *end_line, int *end_column)
  Returns: 1 on success, 0 on failure.
  Sets output parameters; sets to 0 when information unavailable.

  ## co_lnotab (deprecated)

  Deprecated in 3.10, scheduled for removal in 3.12.
  Was: compact bytecode-to-line mapping.
  Now lazily created for backward compat.
  Tools MUST use co_lines() instead.

  ## dis.findlinestarts(code) — Python-level API

  def findlinestarts(code):
      lastline = False
      for start, end, line in code.co_lines():
          if line is not lastline:
              lastline = line
              yield start, line
  Yields (offset, lineno) for each new source line.
  Only yields when line changes (not every instruction).
  Uses co_lines() which returns None for instructions without source.

  ## Gotchas for pflow

  1. Positions can be None: some instructions (especially compiler-generated
     ones like RESUME, PUSH_EXC_INFO, POP_EXCEPT) may have no source position.
     instr.positions may have all-None fields.

  2. co_positions() yields one tuple per CACHE instruction too.
     When iterating with CACHE shown, must consume extra position tuples.
     dis._get_instructions_bytes does: for _ in range(caches): next(co_positions, ())

  3. starts_line semantics:
     3.12: starts_line = int|None (the line number itself or None)
     3.13+: starts_line = bool; line_number = int|None

  4. For 3.12, to get current line of an instruction:
     Track last seen starts_line value across iteration.
     Or use dict(findlinestarts(co)) and check if offset in that dict.
<<<END>>>

