id: 14
ts: 2026-05-28T00:14:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: bytecode library — ControlFlowGraph, TryBegin/TryEnd, ExceptionTableEntry API
authors: Florent Xicluna, Victor Stinner, et al.
year: 2024
venue: bytecode.readthedocs.io/en/latest/api.html
quality: DOCS
url: https://bytecode.readthedocs.io/en/latest/api.html
keywords: BasicBlock, ControlFlowGraph, TryBegin, TryEnd, ExceptionTableEntry, Instr, is_final, from_bytecode
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## BasicBlock

  A straight-line code sequence with no branches in except at entry, no branches
  out except at exit.
  Fields:
    next_block — next BasicBlock or None

  Methods:
    legalize(first_lineno)  — validates instructions, removes SetLineno
    get_jump()              — returns target BasicBlock or None
    get_trailing_end(index) — returns first TryEnd after position

  ## ControlFlowGraph (CFG)

  Inherits from BaseBytecode; comprises BasicBlock list.

  Key methods:
    from_bytecode(bytecode)   — converts labels to blocks, splits after final/conditional jumps
    add_block(instructions)   — creates new BasicBlock
    split_block(block, index) — splits block at instruction, returns new block
    get_dead_blocks()         — retrieves unreachable blocks
    to_bytecode()             — converts back to label-based representation
    compute_stacksize()       — calculates required stack depth

  ## Instr class

  Constructor: Instr(name, arg, lineno=None, location=None)
  Fields:
    name     — operation name (str)
    arg      — varies by type (Label, BasicBlock, CellVar, FreeVar, str, int, Compare)
    lineno   — int|None|UNSET
    location — InstrLocation|None (lineno, end_lineno, col_offset, end_col_offset)
    opcode   — int

  Methods:
    has_jump()       — True if instruction has jump target
    is_cond_jump()   — True if conditional jump
    is_uncond_jump() — True if unconditional jump
    is_final()       — True for RETURN_VALUE, RAISE_VARARGS, unconditional jumps

  ## TryBegin (pseudo-instruction for exception handler entry)

  Fields:
    target      — Label or BasicBlock for exception jump destination
    push_lasti  — bool: whether to push instruction offset before exception handler
    stack_depth — int|UNSET: stack depth restored when handling exception

  ## TryEnd (pseudo-instruction marking end of protected region)

  Fields:
    entry — TryBegin: references corresponding begin

  TryEnd can appear after final instructions or at block start after conditional jumps.

  ## ExceptionTableEntry (concrete level)

  Fields:
    start_offset  — instruction byte offset (inclusive start)
    stop_offset   — instruction byte offset (inclusive stop)
    target        — handler block offset
    stack_depth   — exception context stack depth
    push_lasti    — bool: push lasti before handler

  ## from_bytecode() behavior

  Converts label-based bytecode to CFG by:
    - Converting labels to block pointers
    - Splitting after final and conditional jump instructions
  Result: each BasicBlock ends with either a final instruction, a conditional jump,
  or falls through to next_block.

  ## Exception handling in CFG

  Represented via TryBegin/TryEnd pairs in the instruction stream.
  TryBegin.target points to the exception handler block.
  After assembling: TryBegin/TryEnd pairs converted to exception table entries.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 15
ts: 2026-05-28T00:15:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: CPython zero-cost exception handling — bpo-40222 / gh-84403 and implementation details
authors: Mark Shannon (primary), Python core developers
year: 2021-2022
venue: github.com/python/cpython/issues/84403, github.com/python/cpython/pull/25729
quality: CODE
url: https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/84403
keywords: zero-cost exceptions, SETUP_FINALLY removed, exception table PEP 3.11, co_exceptiontable, lasti bit, depth field
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## Zero-cost exception handling concept (implemented in 3.11)

  Key idea: Replace SETUP_FINALLY/POP_BLOCK pseudo-instructions with metadata.
  This metadata = exception table in co_exceptiontable.
  Entering a try block costs 0 instructions (previously cost 2: SETUP_FINALLY + stack).
  Exception lookup at runtime only happens when exception actually occurs.

  ## Implementation: SETUP_FINALLY → exception table entry

  Before (3.10):
    SETUP_FINALLY  L
    <try body>
    POP_BLOCK
    <normal continuation>
    L:
    <exception handler>

  After (3.11+):
    <try body>       ← protected by exception table entry: (start, end, L, depth, lasti)
    <normal continuation>
    L:               ← exception handler (block leader)
    <exception handler>

  SETUP_FINALLY and POP_BLOCK no longer appear in compiled bytecode.
  They remain as pseudo-instructions in the compiler's intermediate representation.

  ## Stack on exception handler entry (3.11+)

  When exception occurs at instruction I:
    1. Stack is popped to depth D (from exception table entry's depth field)
    2. If lasti=True: push I's bytecode offset (for RERAISE in finally)
    3. Push the exception object
    4. Jump to target T

  So handler block receives: [...stack at depth D..., (lasti,) exception]
  PUSH_EXC_INFO at handler start further adjusts the exception stack.

  ## Stack depth (depth field) semantics

  depth = number of values on stack at entry to the try block (before the try).
  On exception: stack is truncated to this depth, then lasti/exception pushed on top.

  ## lasti field semantics

  lasti=True: The exception handler needs to know which instruction raised,
              so the offset of the raising instruction is pushed before the exception.
  Used in finally blocks so RERAISE can restore f_lasti to point to original raise site.

  ## Impact on CPython interpreter (ceval.c)

  When exception occurs:
    get_exception_handler(frame, offset) → searches co_exceptiontable
    Returns (target, depth, lasti) if found, else propagates to caller.
    Implemented as binary search or linear scan over exception table entries.

  ## Exception table entry ordering

  Entries sorted by start offset.
  For nested try blocks: inner entries appear before outer entries (more specific handler first).
  At runtime: first matching entry (where start <= offset < end) is used.
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 16
ts: 2026-05-28T00:16:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Python/assemble.c — assemble_exception_table, varint encoding implementation
authors: Python core developers
year: 2026
venue: raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Python/assemble.c
quality: CODE
url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Python/assemble.c
keywords: assemble_exception_table, assemble_emit_exception_table_entry, assemble_emit_exception_table_item, varint, CONTINUATION_BIT, write_except_byte, _PyExceptHandlerInfo
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## assemble_exception_table (verbatim)

  static int
  assemble_exception_table(struct assembler *a, instr_sequence *instrs)
  {
      int ioffset = 0;
      _PyExceptHandlerInfo handler;
      handler.h_label = -1;
      handler.h_startdepth = -1;
      handler.h_preserve_lasti = -1;
      int start = -1;
      for (int i = 0; i < instrs->s_used; i++) {
          instruction *instr = &instrs->s_instrs[i];
          if (instr->i_except_handler_info.h_label != handler.h_label) {
              if (handler.h_label >= 0) {
                  int handler_offset = instrs->s_instrs[handler.h_label].i_offset;
                  RETURN_IF_ERROR(
                      assemble_emit_exception_table_entry(a, start, ioffset,
                                                          handler_offset,
                                                          &handler));
              }
              start = ioffset;
              handler = instr->i_except_handler_info;
          }
          ioffset += instr_size(instr);
      }
      if (handler.h_label >= 0) {
          int handler_offset = instrs->s_instrs[handler.h_label].i_offset;
          RETURN_IF_ERROR(assemble_emit_exception_table_entry(a, start, ioffset,
                                                              handler_offset,
                                                              &handler));
      }
      return SUCCESS;
  }

  Logic: Iterates through instructions detecting handler boundary changes.
  When handler changes: emit previous region's exception table entry.
  Entry covers [start, ioffset) with handler at handler_offset.

  ## assemble_emit_exception_table_entry (verbatim)

  static int
  assemble_emit_exception_table_entry(struct assembler *a, int start, int end,
                                      int handler_offset,
                                      _PyExceptHandlerInfo *handler)
  {
      ...
      int size = end - start;
      assert(end > start);
      int target = handler_offset;
      int depth = handler->h_startdepth - 1;
      if (handler->h_preserve_lasti > 0) {
          depth -= 1;
      }
      assert(depth >= 0);
      int depth_lasti = (depth<<1) | handler->h_preserve_lasti;
      assemble_emit_exception_table_item(a, start, (1<<7));   // first byte: MSB set
      assemble_emit_exception_table_item(a, size, 0);
      assemble_emit_exception_table_item(a, target, 0);
      assemble_emit_exception_table_item(a, depth_lasti, 0);
      return SUCCESS;
  }

  CRITICAL NOTE: depth_lasti = (depth<<1) | handler->h_preserve_lasti
  where:
    depth = h_startdepth - 1 (minus another 1 if lasti preserved)
    h_preserve_lasti maps to lasti bit in exception table entry

  The first call passes (1<<7) = 0x80 = MSB for the start field.
  Other fields pass 0 for msb (no start-of-entry marker on continuation fields).

  ## assemble_emit_exception_table_item (verbatim)

  #define CONTINUATION_BIT 64

  static void
  assemble_emit_exception_table_item(struct assembler *a, int value, int msb)
  {
      assert ((msb | 128) == 128);
      assert(value >= 0 && value < (1 << 30));
      if (value >= 1 << 24) {
          write_except_byte(a, (value >> 24) | CONTINUATION_BIT | msb);
          msb = 0;
      }
      if (value >= 1 << 18) {
          write_except_byte(a, ((value >> 18)&0x3f) | CONTINUATION_BIT | msb);
          msb = 0;
      }
      if (value >= 1 << 12) {
          write_except_byte(a, ((value >> 12)&0x3f) | CONTINUATION_BIT | msb);
          msb = 0;
      }
      if (value >= 1 << 6) {
          write_except_byte(a, ((value >> 6)&0x3f) | CONTINUATION_BIT | msb);
          msb = 0;
      }
      write_except_byte(a, (value&0x3f) | msb);
  }

  Byte format for each byte written:
    bit 7 (0x80): MSB/start-of-entry marker — only set for FIRST byte of start field
    bit 6 (0x40 = CONTINUATION_BIT): set if more bytes follow for this value
    bits 5-0 (0x3f): 6 data bits

  Max value per field: 2^30 (30 bits across up to 5 bytes of 6 data bits each)

  ## Relationship between C encoding and Python _parse_varint decoding

  C encoder (assemble_emit_exception_table_item):
    - Groups value into 6-bit chunks from high to low
    - Sets continuation bit (bit 6) on all but last byte
    - First byte of "start" field gets MSB (bit 7) set

  Python decoder (_parse_varint):
    b = next(iterator)
    val = b & 63      # strip both bit 6 and bit 7
    while b & 64:     # continuation bit = bit 6
        val <<= 6
        b = next(iterator)
        val |= b & 63
    return val

  The decoder ignores bit 7 (MSB) — it was the entry-start marker used for
  entry boundary detection at the C level, but Python's _parse_exception_table
  uses sequential parsing (reading exactly 4 varints per entry) rather than
  marker-based parsing. So the MSB on the "start" field is effectively ignored
  by the Python-level parser — it just gets stripped by `b & 63`.
<<<END>>>

