id: 23
ts: 2026-05-28T00:23:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: CPython compiler.md — compilation pipeline stages
authors: Python core developers
year: 2026
venue: raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/InternalDocs/compiler.md
quality: DOCS
url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/InternalDocs/compiler.md
keywords: compilation pipeline, CFG construction, pseudo-instructions, exception table assembly, _PyCfg_FromInstructionSequence, _PyAssemble_MakeCodeObject
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## Compilation pipeline stages (CPython 3.12+)

  Stage 1: Tokenization (lexer/tokenizer)
  Stage 2: Parsing into AST using PEG parser
  Stage 3: AST transformation to instruction sequence via compiler_codegen()
    - codegen stage emits pseudo-instructions (SETUP_FINALLY, SETUP_CLEANUP, SETUP_WITH, POP_BLOCK)
    - Pseudo-instructions never appear in final bytecode
    - "Similar to bytecode, but in some cases more abstract, resolved later"
  Stage 4: CFG construction and optimization
    - _PyCfg_FromInstructionSequence() — converts linear instruction seq to CFG
    - _PyCfg_OptimizeCodeUnit() — optimizes the CFG
  Stage 5: Bytecode emission and code object creation
    - _PyAssemble_MakeCodeObject() — emits bytecode + builds exception table

  ## Basic block definition (from compiler.md)

  "A sequence of bytecode instructions that always execute sequentially"
  "A single entry point at the beginning and a single exit point at the end"
  "If bytecode instruction a needs to jump to instruction b, then a must occur at
   end of its basic block, and b must occur at start of its basic block."

  ## CFG notes

  No detailed algorithm given in compiler.md for identifying block leaders.
  _PyCfg_FromInstructionSequence() does the heavy lifting internally.
  Block leaders come from: jump targets + exception handler targets.

  ## compile.c / codegen.c split (3.12+)

  compile.c: compiler state management, frame blocks
  codegen.c: actual code generation functions (compiler_with, compiler_try_finally, etc.)
  Split motivated by maintainability; preserves commit history via separate file creation.
  Both in Python/ directory.
  (Issue gh-121404 documents the split)
<<<END>>>

