id: 24
ts: 2026-05-28T00:24:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Summary — try/finally bytecode pattern in 3.11-3.14 (finally duplication confirmed)
authors: Various CPython sources
year: 2025
venue: multiple CPython sources (compiler.md, python-dev list, bytecode library docs)
quality: DOCS
url: https://github.com/python/cpython
keywords: try/finally duplication, finally body duplicated, exception path copy, normal path copy, RERAISE, PUSH_EXC_INFO, POP_EXCEPT
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## try/finally bytecode pattern in 3.11-3.14

  CONFIRMED from multiple sources: CPython DUPLICATES the finally body.

  Pattern for:
    try:
        <body>
    finally:
        <finally_body>

  Compiled to (simplified, 3.11+):
    Offset 0:
      <body>                         ← protected by exception table entry

    Offset N (normal exit):
      <finally_body copy 1>          ← executes when body exits normally
      JUMP <after>

    Offset M (exception handler):    ← exception table entry target
      PUSH_EXC_INFO                  ← block leader (is_jump_target=True via exception table)
      <finally_body copy 2>          ← executes when exception raised in body
      RERAISE 1                      ← re-raises with saved f_lasti

  Exception table entry:
    start=0, end=N, target=M, depth=<stack_depth>, lasti=True

  ## try/except pattern in 3.11-3.14

  Pattern for:
    try:
        <body>
    except ExcType as e:
        <handler>

  Compiled to (simplified, 3.11+):
    Offset 0:
      <body>                         ← protected by exception table entry

    Offset N:                        ← fallthrough after body (no exception)
      JUMP <after>

    Offset M:                        ← exception handler (exception table target)
      PUSH_EXC_INFO
      LOAD <ExcType>
      CHECK_EXC_MATCH
      POP_JUMP_IF_FALSE <L_no_match>
      <handler>
      POP_EXCEPT
      JUMP <after>
    L_no_match:
      RERAISE 0                      ← no handler matched; propagate

  Exception table entry:
    start=0, end=N, target=M, depth=<stack_depth>, lasti=False (for except)
    or lasti=True (for finally -- to preserve f_lasti for RERAISE 1)

  ## try/except/finally pattern

  try:
    <body>
  except ExcType:
    <handler>
  finally:
    <finally_body>

  Compiled as NESTED try blocks:
    Inner try: body with except handler
    Outer try: inner-try with finally
  Results in multiple exception table entries (one per try block level).

  ## with-statement pattern in 3.12/3.13

  with ctx as x:
    <body>

  BEFORE_WITH              ← loads __exit__, calls __enter__, pushes result
  STORE <x>
  <body>                   ← protected by exception table entry (lasti=True)

  Exception handler:       ← exception table target
    PUSH_EXC_INFO
    WITH_EXCEPT_START      ← calls __exit__(exc_type, exc_val, exc_tb)
    POP_JUMP_IF_TRUE L     ← if __exit__ returns truthy: suppress exception
    RERAISE 1
  L:
    POP_TOP                ← pop __exit__ result
    POP_EXCEPT
    (continue after with)

  ## with-statement pattern in 3.14

  (LOAD_SPECIAL-based; see record 22)
  LOAD_SPECIAL 1           ← loads __enter__
  CALL + STORE <x>
  LOAD_SPECIAL 0           ← loads __exit__ (prep for handler use)
  <body>                   ← protected by exception table entry

  Exception handler: same structure (PUSH_EXC_INFO + WITH_EXCEPT_START + ...)

  ## Key CFG implications

  For pflow CFG construction:
  1. Exception table entries create edges from ALL instructions in [start, end) to target.
  2. Target instruction is ALWAYS a block leader (first instruction of handler block).
  3. PUSH_EXC_INFO at handler entry: block leader by virtue of being exception table target.
  4. RERAISE terminates a block (unconditionally).
  5. POP_JUMP_IF_TRUE in with-handler: creates two edges (suppress vs. reraise path).
  6. Finally blocks: appear TWICE in bytecode. Both copies are normal basic blocks.
     The second copy (exception path) has block leader = exception table target.
  7. Nested try blocks: nested exception table entries; inner handler covered by outer entry.
<<<END>>>


