id: 26
ts: 2026-05-28T00:26:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Python 3.13 dis.py source — Instruction class (12 fields + properties), _make_labels_map
authors: Python core developers
year: 2024
venue: raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/v3.13.0/Lib/dis.py
quality: CODE
url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/v3.13.0/Lib/dis.py
keywords: Instruction 3.13 fields, label field, _make_labels_map, starts_line bool, line_number, jump_target property, cache_info
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## Python 3.13 Instruction namedtuple (verbatim)

  _Instruction = collections.namedtuple(
      "_Instruction",
      [
          'opname', 'opcode', 'arg', 'argval', 'argrepr',
          'offset', 'start_offset', 'starts_line', 'line_number',
          'label', 'positions', 'cache_info',
      ],
      defaults=[None, None, None]
  )

  FIELDS IN 3.13 (12 total, class-based with properties):
    opname, opcode, arg, argval, argrepr
    offset          — byte offset of this instruction
    start_offset    — byte offset including EXTENDED_ARG prefixes [NEW in 3.13]
    starts_line     — bool: True if opcode starts a new source line [CHANGED from int|None]
    line_number     — int|None: current source line number [NEW in 3.13]
    label           — logical label string for jump display (e.g. "L1") [NEW field]
    positions       — dis.Positions [was in 3.12 too, added 3.11]
    cache_info      — list of (name, size, data) tuples [NEW in 3.13]

  Properties (computed, not stored in namedtuple):
    oparg           — alias for arg
    baseopcode      — _deoptop(self.opcode)
    baseopname      — opname[self.baseopcode]
    cache_offset    — self.offset + 2
    end_offset      — self.cache_offset + _get_cache_size(...)*2
    jump_target     — _get_jump_target(self.opcode, self.arg, self.offset)
    is_jump_target  — self.label is not None  [now a PROPERTY, not stored field!]

  ## _make_labels_map (3.13+ specific)

  Creates mapping from byte offset → logical label.
  Called with (original_code, exception_entries) — includes BOTH:
    - Jump targets from _get_jump_target
    - Exception handler targets from exception_entries

  This is why exception handler block leaders also get labels and
  is_jump_target=True: they appear in the labels_map.

  ## Key behavior change for is_jump_target in 3.13

  In 3.12: is_jump_target stored as field in namedtuple; computed from findlabels().
  In 3.13+: is_jump_target is a PROPERTY: return self.label is not None
            Labels include both jump targets AND exception handler targets.
  Result: In 3.13+, instructions targeted by exception table ALSO have is_jump_target=True.
  In 3.12: is_jump_target covers ONLY bytecode jump targets (not exception table targets).

  PFLOW IMPLICATION:
    In 3.12, to find ALL block leaders:
      1. instr.is_jump_target = True (jump targets)
      2. Also check exception_entries targets (not covered by is_jump_target in 3.12)
    In 3.13+:
      instr.is_jump_target = True covers BOTH jump targets AND exception table targets.
      (Because _make_labels_map includes exception_entries targets in label computation.)
      (Or equivalently: instr.label is not None)
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 27
ts: 2026-05-28T00:27:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: LOAD_FAST_BORROW opcode — Python 3.14 reference-counting optimization
authors: Python core developers
year: 2025
venue: github.com/python/cpython/issues/130704 + rushter.com/blog/python-refcount/
quality: DOCS
url: https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/130704
keywords: LOAD_FAST_BORROW, borrowed reference, reference counting, opcode 3.14, LOAD_FAST_BORROW_LOAD_FAST_BORROW
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## LOAD_FAST_BORROW (Python 3.14)

  New opcode added in 3.14.
  Loads a local variable WITHOUT incrementing its reference count.
  Uses a "borrowed reference" that is tagged to avoid refcount operations.
  Safe when the reference in the frame outlives the reference loaded onto the stack.

  LOAD_FAST vs LOAD_FAST_BORROW:
    LOAD_FAST: load local, increment refcount (INCREF)
    LOAD_FAST_BORROW: load local, tag as borrowed (no INCREF; no DECREF when consumed)
  
  Borrowed refs loaded onto operand stack live until end of consuming instruction.
  Any borrowed ref that would escape to heap (frame object, generator) → converted to strong ref.
  
  Performance: "Eliminating 90% of refcounts in LOAD_FAST = 2-3% speedup in interpreter."
  NOT a basic-block terminator.

  LOAD_FAST_BORROW_LOAD_FAST_BORROW — optimized variant loading two locals in one op.
  NOT a basic-block terminator.

  ## RESUME opcode semantics (3.12-3.14)

  RESUME is the first instruction in every function/generator/coroutine.
  NOT a basic-block terminator; it's the entry point but doesn't jump.

  oparg interpretation:
    3.12:
      oparg bits 1-0 (context):
        0 = start of module/class/function
        1 = after yield
        2 = after yield from
        3 = after await
    3.13 CHANGE:
      "The oparg value changed to include information about except-depth"
      oparg bits 1-0: same context bits as 3.12
      oparg bit 2: 1 if RESUME is at except-depth 1, 0 otherwise
      (Used to optimize closing of generators)

  ## bytecode library version changes (0.15-0.17)

  0.15.0 (Python 3.12 support):
    LOAD_ATTR changed: arg = tuple[bool, str] (low bit = is_method)
    LOAD_SUPER_ATTR: arg = tuple[bool, bool, str]
    POP_JUMP_IF_*: now undirected (no forward/backward distinction at library level)
    YIELD_VALUE: now requires arg

  0.16.0 (Python 3.13 support):
    Comparison ops: new enum variants (LT_CAST, LE_CAST, etc.)
    LOAD_FAST: now accesses free and cell vars

  0.17.0 (Python 3.14 support):
    NEW: BUILD_TEMPLATE, BUILD_INTERPOLATION, LOAD_SMALL_INT
         LOAD_FAST_BORROW, LOAD_FAST_BORROW_LOAD_FAST_BORROW
         LOAD_COMMON_CONSTANT, LOAD_SPECIAL
    LOAD_SPECIAL: arg = SpecialMethod enum (dedicated enum for oparg values)
    BINARY_OP: now uses enum supporting subscript operations
    CONVERT_VALUE: uses FormatValue enum
    CFG bugfix: ControlFlowGraph.split_block() now sets next_block correctly
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 28
ts: 2026-05-28T00:28:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Python 3.12 dis.findlinestarts vs 3.13 — behavioral difference for None lines
authors: Python core developers
year: 2023-2024
venue: raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython (multiple versions)
quality: CODE
url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython
keywords: findlinestarts difference, co_lines None, starts_line None vs False, line tracking across versions
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## findlinestarts behavioral difference across versions

  Python 3.12 (from source):
    def findlinestarts(code):
        lastline = None
        for start, end, line in code.co_lines():
            if line is not None and line != lastline:
                lastline = line
                yield start, line
        return
    Skips None lines (no yield when co_lines() returns None for a range).

  Python 3.13+ (from source):
    def findlinestarts(code):
        lastline = False
        for start, end, line in code.co_lines():
            if line is not lastline:
                lastline = line
                yield start, line
        return
    Yields even when line is None (but only when it changes — first None range yields None).
    Uses `lastline = False` (sentinel) so first iteration always yields.

  ## Impact on line tracking for CFG construction

  In 3.12: To track current line of an instruction:
    linestarts = dict(findlinestarts(co))  # maps offset → line number (no None entries)
    Iterate instructions; track last seen line from linestarts.
    instr.starts_line != None → new line.
    instr.starts_line value = the line number.

  In 3.13+: To track current line:
    instr.starts_line == True → new line starting at this instruction
    instr.line_number → the current line number (int or None)
    Both always populated.

  ## co_lines() semantics (identical across 3.12-3.14)

  code.co_lines() → iterator of (start, end, line)
    start: byte offset (inclusive)
    end: byte offset (exclusive)
    line: int line number OR None
  Covers entire bytecode span [0, len(bytecode)).
  Consecutive, non-decreasing ranges.
  Line=None: instructions without source association (e.g., RESUME, PUSH_EXC_INFO).

  ## Instruction.positions for None-position instructions

  For instructions without source location (e.g., RESUME at function start,
  PUSH_EXC_INFO at exception handler entry):
    instr.positions may be a Positions object with all-None fields:
      Positions(lineno=None, end_lineno=None, col_offset=None, end_col_offset=None)
    or positions itself may be None if co_positions iterator was exhausted.

  ## co_positions() indexing note

  list(code.co_positions())[i] = position of i-th instruction (including CACHE entries).
  To get position of instruction at byte offset O:
    instruction_index = O // 2  (each instruction = 2 bytes)
    But must account for CACHE entries!
    positions[O//2] includes CACHE entries in the count.
    Use co.co_positions() and co.co_lines() together, not raw offset division.
  
  Correct approach: iterate dis.get_instructions() which handles cache skipping.
  Each yielded Instruction has .positions already set correctly.
  
  Alternative: iterate _get_instructions_bytes which calls next(co_positions, ())
    for each cache entry to skip, then assigns positions to the instruction.
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 29
ts: 2026-05-28T00:29:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Finally block duplication in CPython — history and current status
authors: Mark Shannon, Guido van Rossum, Serhiy Storchaka (issue 33387), CPython developers
year: 2018-2025
venue: bugs.python.org/issue33387
quality: DOCS
url: https://bugs.python.org/issue33387
keywords: finally block duplication, code duplication, try-finally, exception path copy, normal path copy, Python 3.9+
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## Finally block code duplication — CONFIRMED in CPython

  The solution in bpo-33387 (merged Nov 2019, landed in Python 3.9) EXPLICITLY chose
  code duplication over complex control flow opcodes.

  Pattern from issue:
    SETUP_FINALLY except
    try-body
    POP_BLOCK
    finally-body       ← COPY 1 (normal path)
    JUMP exit
    except:
    finally-body       ← COPY 2 (exception path)
    exit:

  Quote: "generate separate code for normal and exceptional paths by implementing
  finally blocks by code duplication."

  This design decision was intentional: simpler opcodes, larger code size.

  ## Status in 3.11-3.14

  The duplication pattern PERSISTS in 3.11-3.14.
  SETUP_FINALLY was removed from final bytecode (became pseudo-instruction in 3.11).
  The exception table in co_exceptiontable maps the try-body → exception-path-copy.
  Normal path copy: executed via fallthrough after try-body exits normally.
  Exception path copy: jumped to via exception table lookup.

  EVIDENCE: "CPython duplicates the code for a finally clause" (python-dev mailing list).
  EVIDENCE: bytecode library docs mention "stack size computed before duplication"
            implying duplication still occurs.

  ## Layout optimization (3.12+)

  Issue gh-93356: "Lay out exception handling code at end of code unit"
  Exception handler blocks (cold blocks) moved to end of bytecode.
  The finally-exception-copy appears after the normal-exit code.
  This is a layout optimization, not removal of duplication.

  ## Impact on pflow CFG construction

  When building CFG from bytecode:
    1. Two copies of finally body = two separate sequences of basic blocks.
    2. The exception-path copy is a basic block starting at the exception table target.
    3. Exception table target → PUSH_EXC_INFO → <finally copy 2> → RERAISE.
    4. Normal path: <finally copy 1> → JUMP after.
    5. Both copies have distinct offsets; no sharing at bytecode level.
    6. CFG must treat them as separate blocks (they are separate).
    7. No need to "merge" them — each copy is structurally independent.

  ## RERAISE oparg in finally context

  RERAISE 1 (oparg=1): appears at end of exception-path finally copy.
    Pops lasti from stack, sets f_lasti, then re-raises exception.
    The lasti was pushed by the exception table handler entry (lasti=True).
  RERAISE 0 (oparg=0): appears in except handlers (no lasti restoration needed).
<<<END>>>

