id: 31
ts: 2026-05-28T00:31:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: EXTENDED_ARG handling and HAVE_ARGUMENT threshold across 3.12-3.14
authors: Python core developers + community
year: 2023-2025
venue: CPython source, python docs, laike9m.com/blog/demystifying-extended_arg,124/
quality: DOCS
url: https://docs.python.org/3/library/dis.html
keywords: EXTENDED_ARG, argument accumulation, HAVE_ARGUMENT, dis.hasarg, 3.12 hasarg, argument-less opcodes
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## EXTENDED_ARG opcode

  EXTENDED_ARG = 144 (numeric value, stable across 3.12-3.14).
  Purpose: extend the 8-bit argument to larger values.
  Format: up to 3 EXTENDED_ARG instructions can precede any instruction.
  Argument accumulation: extended_arg = (extended_arg << 8) | instr.arg
    Each EXTENDED_ARG shifts left 8 bits and ORs with next byte.
    Final instruction gets (accumulated_arg | its_own_arg).
  Max argument with 3 EXTENDED_ARGs: 32-bit value.

  ## HAVE_ARGUMENT threshold

  Value: 90 in 3.12 and earlier.
  Meaning: real opcodes with opcode >= 90 have an argument; < 90 do not.
  DEPRECATED since 3.13: use dis.hasarg instead.
  In 3.12: HAVE_ARGUMENT still meaningful for real (non-pseudo) opcodes.

  ## dis.hasarg (added 3.12)

  Preferred alternative to HAVE_ARGUMENT check.
  Contains all opcodes (including pseudo instructions) that use their argument.
  Use: instr.opname in dis.hasarg  (or opcode in dis.hasarg)

  ## Argument-handling in _unpack_opargs

  From dis.py (both 3.12 and 3.13+):
  extended_arg = 0
  for i in range(0, len(code), 2):
      op = code[i]
      deop = _deoptop(op)
      if deop in hasarg:
          arg = code[i+1] | extended_arg
          extended_arg = (arg << 8) if deop == EXTENDED_ARG else 0
      else:
          arg = None
          extended_arg = 0

  IMPORTANT: EXTENDED_ARG instructions ARE yielded by _unpack_opargs.
  In dis.get_instructions():
    EXTENDED_ARG: start_offset stays the same as offset (no accumulated prefix).
    The next real instruction: start_offset = first EXTENDED_ARG's offset.
    So instr.start_offset != instr.offset when EXTENDED_ARG prefixes are present.

  ## Opcodes without arguments (opcode < 90, 3.12)

  Common ones relevant to CFG:
    PUSH_EXC_INFO   = 35 (no arg)
    CHECK_EXC_MATCH = 36 (no arg)
    WITH_EXCEPT_START = 49 (no arg)
    BEFORE_WITH     = 53 (no arg — but SETS UP exception handler)
    POP_EXCEPT      = 89 (no arg)

  Wait: the earlier fetch showed BEFORE_WITH = 53 (no arg in opcode.py)
  but it HAS an oparg for stack depth. Clarification needed.
  [NOTE: BEFORE_WITH in 3.12 opcode.py listed as 53. Its oparg = lasti preservation flag.]

  ## Argument handling for CFG jump computation (key summary)

  When building CFG:
  1. Use dis.get_instructions() — EXTENDED_ARG handling is automatic.
     Each Instruction.arg is already the accumulated full argument.
     instr.start_offset points to first EXTENDED_ARG prefix.
  2. Don't iterate co_code bytes directly for jumps — offsets include CACHE and EXTENDED_ARG.
  3. Jump target formula (all relative jumps in 3.12-3.14):
     target = instr.offset + 2 + instr.arg * 2 + 2 * cache_size
     For backward jumps: arg is used as-is (negative sign handled by subtracting not adding)
     (See _get_jump_target in dis.py for authoritative formula)
  4. In 3.13+: just use instr.jump_target (property; already computed).
<<<END>>>

