id: 35
ts: 2026-05-28T00:35:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pycore_opcode_utils.h — IS_TERMINATOR_OPCODE, IS_SCOPE_EXIT_OPCODE, IS_UNCONDITIONAL_JUMP_OPCODE (verbatim definitions)
authors: Python core developers
year: 2026
venue: raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Include/internal/pycore_opcode_utils.h
quality: CODE
url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Include/internal/pycore_opcode_utils.h
keywords: IS_TERMINATOR_OPCODE, IS_SCOPE_EXIT_OPCODE, IS_UNCONDITIONAL_JUMP_OPCODE, IS_CONDITIONAL_JUMP_OPCODE, JUMP, JUMP_NO_INTERRUPT, RETURN_VALUE, RERAISE, RAISE_VARARGS
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## IS_TERMINATOR_OPCODE (verbatim)

  #define IS_TERMINATOR_OPCODE(opcode) \
          (OPCODE_HAS_JUMP(opcode) || IS_SCOPE_EXIT_OPCODE(opcode))

  Meaning: a terminator is ANY opcode that has a jump (conditional OR unconditional)
           OR is a scope exit (return/raise).

  ## IS_SCOPE_EXIT_OPCODE (verbatim)

  #define IS_SCOPE_EXIT_OPCODE(opcode) \
          ((opcode) == RETURN_VALUE || \
           (opcode) == RAISE_VARARGS || \
           (opcode) == RERAISE)

  EXACTLY: RETURN_VALUE, RAISE_VARARGS, RERAISE
  NOTE: RETURN_CONST is NOT listed here!
    In 3.12/3.13: RETURN_CONST must also be a scope exit (terminates block).
    Possible explanation: RETURN_CONST was a pseudo-instruction in codegen or
    was added separately. Check if RETURN_CONST was present at time of this macro.
    ALTERNATIVELY: RETURN_CONST may have been converted to RETURN_VALUE before
    the flow graph check, or IS_SCOPE_EXIT_OPCODE was updated for 3.12.
    [OPEN QUESTION: Is IS_SCOPE_EXIT_OPCODE exactly as shown for 3.12/3.13?]

  ## IS_UNCONDITIONAL_JUMP_OPCODE (verbatim)

  #define IS_UNCONDITIONAL_JUMP_OPCODE(opcode) \
          ((opcode) == JUMP || \
           (opcode) == JUMP_NO_INTERRUPT || \
           (opcode) == JUMP_FORWARD || \
           (opcode) == JUMP_BACKWARD || \
           (opcode) == JUMP_BACKWARD_NO_INTERRUPT)

  EXACTLY:
    JUMP                  — pseudo-instruction (appears only in intermediate rep)
    JUMP_NO_INTERRUPT     — pseudo-instruction
    JUMP_FORWARD          — real opcode
    JUMP_BACKWARD         — real opcode
    JUMP_BACKWARD_NO_INTERRUPT — real opcode

  JUMP and JUMP_NO_INTERRUPT: pseudo-instructions used during flowgraph/codegen;
    replaced by JUMP_FORWARD or JUMP_BACKWARD in final bytecode.

  ## IS_CONDITIONAL_JUMP_OPCODE (verbatim)

  #define IS_CONDITIONAL_JUMP_OPCODE(opcode) \
          ((opcode) == POP_JUMP_IF_FALSE || \
           (opcode) == POP_JUMP_IF_TRUE || \
           (opcode) == POP_JUMP_IF_NONE || \
           (opcode) == POP_JUMP_IF_NOT_NONE)

  EXACTLY: POP_JUMP_IF_FALSE, POP_JUMP_IF_TRUE, POP_JUMP_IF_NONE, POP_JUMP_IF_NOT_NONE

  NOTE: FOR_ITER and SEND are NOT listed here but ARE conditional terminators
  at the Python-visible bytecode level.
  FOR_ITER jumps on iterator exhaustion; SEND jumps on StopIteration.
  They may be handled separately in flowgraph analysis.

  ## check_cfg() (verbatim — uses IS_TERMINATOR_OPCODE)

  static int
  check_cfg(cfg_builder *g) {
      for (basicblock *b = g->g_entryblock; b != NULL; b = b->b_next) {
          for (int i = 0; i < b->b_iused; i++) {
              int opcode = b->b_instr[i].i_opcode;
              assert(!IS_ASSEMBLER_OPCODE(opcode));
              if (IS_TERMINATOR_OPCODE(opcode)) {
                  if (i != b->b_iused - 1) {
                      PyErr_SetString(PyExc_SystemError,
                          "malformed control flow graph.");
                      return ERROR;
                  }
              }
          }
      }
      return SUCCESS;
  }

  Validates: all terminator opcodes appear ONLY at the last position in their block.

  ## #includes in flowgraph.c (from assemble.c fetch)

  #include "pycore_opcode_utils.h"    ← defines IS_TERMINATOR_OPCODE etc.
  #include "pycore_opcode_metadata.h" ← defines IS_PSEUDO_INSTR, opcode flags

  ## Summary: real-bytecode terminators (for Python-level CFG from dis)

  SCOPE EXITS (IS_SCOPE_EXIT_OPCODE): RETURN_VALUE, RAISE_VARARGS, RERAISE
  UNCONDITIONAL JUMPS (final bytecode only): JUMP_FORWARD, JUMP_BACKWARD, JUMP_BACKWARD_NO_INTERRUPT
  CONDITIONAL jumps (two successors): POP_JUMP_IF_FALSE, POP_JUMP_IF_TRUE,
    POP_JUMP_IF_NONE, POP_JUMP_IF_NOT_NONE, FOR_ITER, SEND

  In final compiled bytecode (dis output), JUMP and JUMP_NO_INTERRUPT never appear
  (they are pseudo-instructions resolved to JUMP_FORWARD or JUMP_BACKWARD).
<<<END>>>


