id: 39
ts: 2026-05-28T00:39:00Z
relevance: MED
title: Discovering basic blocks from CPython bytecode — Max Bernstein
authors: Max Bernstein
year: 2020-2022
venue: bernsteinbear.com/blog/discovering-basic-blocks/
quality: BLOG
url: https://bernsteinbear.com/blog/discovering-basic-blocks/
keywords: basic block algorithm, block leaders, jump target, CODEUNIT_SIZE, CFG construction, two-pass algorithm
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## Two-pass algorithm for identifying basic block leaders

  Pass 1 — Finding block starts:
    leaders = {0}  # index 0 always a leader
    for each instruction:
      if instruction is_branch():
        leaders.add(next_instr_idx())
        leaders.add(jump_target_idx())
      if instruction is return or raise:
        leaders.add(next_instr_idx())  # dead code after return = new block
    Sort leaders.

  Pass 2 — Creating blocks:
    blocks = [instructions[leaders[i]:leaders[i+1]] for consecutive pairs]

  ## Control flow categories (article's 3.6 era, but principles still apply)

  Branch instructions (two successors):
    FOR_ITER, JUMP_ABSOLUTE, JUMP_FORWARD,
    JUMP_IF_FALSE_OR_POP, JUMP_IF_TRUE_OR_POP (removed in 3.12),
    POP_JUMP_IF_FALSE, POP_JUMP_IF_TRUE

  Return instructions (one successor — none): RETURN_VALUE
  Raise instructions (one successor — none): RAISE_VARARGS

  NOTE: Article explicitly did NOT handle exception tables (admitted limitation).
  NOTE: Article targets CPython 3.6+; does not cover 3.11+ exception table changes.

  ## jump_target_idx formula (from article)

  For relative branches: target = next_instr_offset() + arg
  For absolute branches: target = arg
  Convert offset to index: idx = offset // CODEUNIT_SIZE (= offset // 2)

  ## Limitation for 3.11+ bytecode

  Article does not address:
    - Exception table entries as block leaders
    - CACHE entries and their effect on offsets
    - PUSH_EXC_INFO / RERAISE / RESUME opcodes
    - co_exceptiontable parsing

  ## FOR_ITER and SEND as conditional terminators (from doc research)

  FOR_ITER(delta): calls __next__() on iterator.
    Two successors:
      1. Falls through to next instruction (iterator yields value)
      2. Jumps delta ahead (iterator exhausted)
    delta is relative to instruction after CACHE entries.
    Block leader: both fallthrough and jump target.

  SEND(delta): equivalent to stack[-2].send(stack[-1]).
    Two successors:
      1. Falls through (non-StopIteration result)
      2. Jumps delta ahead (StopIteration — push .value)
    Used for yield-from / await.
    Both fallthrough and jump target are block leaders.

  IS_CONDITIONAL_JUMP_OPCODE does NOT include FOR_ITER or SEND.
  But both ARE conditional terminators in practice.
  For pflow: treat FOR_ITER and SEND as conditional terminators separately.
<<<END>>>


