id: 41
ts: 2026-05-28T00:41:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: CLEANUP_THROW opcode — Python 3.12 generator close exception handling
authors: Python core developers (Brandt Bucher, gh-90997)
year: 2022
venue: docs.python.org/3/library/dis.html
quality: DOCS
url: https://docs.python.org/3/library/dis.html#opcode-CLEANUP_THROW
keywords: CLEANUP_THROW, generator close, throw(), StopIteration, exception handling generator, 3.12 opcode
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## CLEANUP_THROW (Python 3.12)

  Added in 3.12. (Contributed by Brandt Bucher in gh-90997)

  Behavior:
    If STACK[-1] is an instance of StopIteration:
      pop three values from stack
      push STACK[-1].value (the StopIteration value)
    Else:
      re-raise STACK[-1]

  Used during throw() or close() calls on a generator/coroutine/async-generator.
  Handles exception raised during these calls in the generator's frame.

  NOT a block terminator in the conventional sense.
  Conditionally terminates depending on exception type:
    StopIteration case: continues (pushes value)
    Other: re-raises (exits current frame)

  For CFG purposes: CLEANUP_THROW is similar to a conditional terminator.
  The re-raise path leads to exception propagation (like RERAISE).
  The StopIteration path falls through to next instruction.

  ## CLEANUP_THROW vs RERAISE

  RERAISE: unconditionally terminates (always re-raises).
  CLEANUP_THROW: conditionally terminates (may re-raise or continue).

  ## Context in generator close/throw

  When a generator's throw() or close() method is called:
    The exception is thrown into the generator frame.
    CLEANUP_THROW handles the case where a StopIteration is raised inside
    a for-loop or yield-from expression within the generator.

  ## pflow implication

  For pflow CFG: CLEANUP_THROW should be treated as a two-successor node
  if precise modeling is needed:
    Successor 1: fallthrough (StopIteration case — value pushed)
    Successor 2: exception propagation (re-raise case — exits)
  Conservative approach: treat as non-terminator (only fallthrough modeled).
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 42
ts: 2026-05-28T00:42:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: CACHE entries, show_caches, and instr.argval semantics for jump targets
authors: Python core developers + community
year: 2023-2024
venue: docs.python.org/3/library/dis.html + python-dev mailing list
quality: DOCS
url: https://docs.python.org/3/library/dis.html
keywords: CACHE offset, show_caches, argval jump target, instr.argval, cache transparent, forward jumps, backward jumps, 3.12 3.13
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## CACHE entries and their effect on iteration

  3.11: CACHE instructions introduced; show_caches=True includes them as separate items.
  3.12: show_caches=True still works; CACHE items included in iteration.
        Jump argument = offset relative to instruction AFTER cache entries.
        Forward jumps: transparent (cache entries after jump, target already past them).
        Backward jumps: cache entries of jump instruction included in offset calculation.
  3.13: show_caches DEPRECATED for get_instructions(); cache info in Instruction.cache_info.
        No separate CACHE Instruction items emitted.

  ## instr.argval for jump instructions

  In 3.12:
    argval = resolved jump target byte offset (int)
    For relative jumps: argval = instr.offset + 2 + instr.arg*2 + 2*cache_size
    For absolute jumps: argval = instr.arg*2
    NOTE: argval IS the byte offset. You can use it directly to find the target instruction.

  In 3.13+:
    argval = "resolved arg value (if any)"
    For jump instructions: argval = target LABEL string (e.g., "L1") NOT an int offset!
    (Because 3.13 shows logical labels in dis output.)
    HOWEVER: instr.jump_target = byte offset (int) ← use this for CFG construction.

  ## Recommended approach for getting jump target offset (all versions)

  def get_jump_target_offset(instr):
      # 3.13+: use .jump_target property directly
      if hasattr(type(instr), 'jump_target') and \
         isinstance(getattr(type(instr), 'jump_target', None), property):
          return instr.jump_target  # int offset or None
      # 3.12 fallback: use argval if it's an int, or dis._get_jump_target
      if instr.opname in dis.hasjrel or instr.opname in (dis.hasjabs or []):
          if isinstance(instr.argval, int):
              return instr.argval
      return None

  Safest approach:
    Use dis._parse_exception_table(co) and _get_jump_target separately.
    Or: always call dis._get_jump_target(instr.opcode, instr.arg, instr.offset).
    Private but stable across 3.12-3.14.

  ## Effect of CACHE on offset counting for block-leader identification

  When iterating dis.get_instructions() with default show_caches=False (all versions):
    Instruction.offset = byte offset of the opcode word (not including CACHE entries).
    CACHE entries are at offset+2, offset+4, etc. but NOT yielded as separate Instruction.
    Next instruction after caches: offset + 2 + 2*cache_count.

  The offsets visible in the Instruction stream are "real" instruction offsets.
  Exception table start/end/target fields are also byte offsets (in same space).
  So: direct comparison works: entry.target == instr.offset (no adjustment needed).

  ## CACHE and show_caches in 3.12 when building block-leaders

  If you use dis.get_instructions(code, show_caches=False) (DEFAULT in 3.12):
    CACHE instructions NOT emitted.
    Instruction offsets skip cache entries (offsets are non-contiguous by 2*caches).
    findlabels() and jump_target computation already account for cache entries.
    So: directly usable for block-leader identification.

  If you use dis.get_instructions(code, show_caches=True) in 3.12:
    CACHE instructions emitted between real instructions.
    Each CACHE has opname='CACHE', its own offset.
    Must filter out CACHE entries when building block-leaders:
      leaders = {instr.offset for instr in instrs if instr.opname != 'CACHE'
                 and instr.is_jump_target}

  RECOMMENDATION: use show_caches=False (default) for CFG construction.
    Simpler; CACHE entries don't affect control flow.
    In 3.13+: show_caches ignored anyway.
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 43
ts: 2026-05-28T00:43:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: BEFORE_WITH opcode description (Python 3.11-3.13) and RETURN_GENERATOR / YIELD_VALUE semantics
authors: Python core developers
year: 2022-2024
venue: docs.python.org/3.13/library/dis.html
quality: DOCS
url: https://docs.python.org/3.13/library/dis.html
keywords: BEFORE_WITH stack effect, __enter__ __exit__ push order, RETURN_GENERATOR, YIELD_VALUE oparg, generator function first opcode
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## BEFORE_WITH (Python 3.11-3.13)

  Official description:
    "This opcode performs several operations before a with block starts.
     First, it loads __exit__() from the context manager and pushes it onto the stack
     for later use by WITH_EXCEPT_START. Then, __enter__() is called. Finally, the
     result of calling the __enter__() method is pushed onto the stack."

  Added in 3.11. Removed in 3.14.

  Stack effect:
    Input:  [..., context_manager]
    Output: [..., __exit__method, __enter__result]
    (context_manager consumed; __exit__ at -2, result at -1)

  Oparg: not documented publicly. From opcode.py: 53 (numeric value).
    From the BEFORE_WITH implementation: similar to SETUP_WITH in older Python.
    No documented oparg meaning (treated as 0).

  Exception table: BEFORE_WITH triggers creation of exception table entry covering
  the with-body. The entry's depth and lasti fields are set so that at exception
  handler entry: __exit__ is at the correct stack position for WITH_EXCEPT_START.

  NOT a basic-block terminator.

  ## BEFORE_ASYNC_WITH (Python 3.11-3.13)

  Async variant of BEFORE_WITH.
  Resolves __aenter__() and __aexit__() from the async context manager.
  Removed in 3.14.

  ## RETURN_GENERATOR (Python 3.11+)

  Added in 3.11.
  Description: "Create a generator, coroutine, or async generator from the current
    frame. Used as first opcode of code objects for the above-mentioned callables.
    Clear the current frame and return the newly created generator."

  Key: RETURN_GENERATOR is the FIRST instruction in any generator/coroutine/async-gen
  function code object. At Python bytecode level, calling a generator function
  executes RETURN_GENERATOR which returns the generator WITHOUT executing the body.
  Subsequent next()/send() calls RESUME from RESUME instructions.

  NOT a basic-block terminator in the CFG of the generator function itself.
  Terminates the "startup" portion and transitions to generator state.

  ## YIELD_VALUE oparg (Python 3.13+)

  3.12: YIELD_VALUE oparg = stack_depth (used internally)
  3.13: YIELD_VALUE oparg = 1 if yield is part of yield-from or await, 0 otherwise.
  "Changed in version 3.11: oparg set to stack depth. Changed in version 3.12: oparg
   set to exception block depth, for efficient closing of generators."

  NOT a basic-block terminator (resumes after yield).

  ## Generator function bytecode structure (3.11+)

  Function containing yield:
    Offset 0: RESUME 0          ← entry point (oparg=0 = function start)
    Wait... RETURN_GENERATOR is FIRST, before RESUME?

  Actually from docs: "Used as first opcode in code objects for generators."
  So code object starts: RETURN_GENERATOR, then RESUME 0, then body...
  When generator is created: RETURN_GENERATOR executes, returns generator.
  When generator.send(None) called: execution continues from RESUME.
  RESUME marks the resumption point after RETURN_GENERATOR.
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 44
ts: 2026-05-28T00:44:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Deciphering CPython 3.13 Bytecode through Experiments — concrete dis output examples
authors: mima_ita
year: 2024
venue: zenn.dev/mima_ita/articles/c425d1ab1602a0
quality: BLOG
url: https://zenn.dev/mima_ita/articles/c425d1ab1602a0?locale=en
keywords: Python 3.13 bytecode example, exception table concrete, LOAD_FAST_LOAD_FAST, FOR_ITER, JUMP_BACKWARD, RETURN_GENERATOR, YIELD_VALUE, exception table structure
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  ## Simple addition function bytecode (Python 3.13)

  def add(x, y): return x + y

  RESUME                   0
  LOAD_FAST_LOAD_FAST      1 (x, y)    ← optimized pair load (3.13 new)
  BINARY_OP                0 (+)
  RETURN_VALUE

  ## Exception table entry format (Python 3.13 concrete)

  For try/except/finally, example exception table:
  | Start | End | Target | Depth | lasti | Purpose |
  | 4     | 26  | 50     | 0     | False | Exception in try block |
  | 50    | 70  | 114    | 1     | True  | Type mismatch handling |
  | 70    | 94  | 104    | 1     | True  | Exception in except body |

  Observations:
    - Multiple entries can cover overlapping/nested ranges
    - Depth can be 0 or more (number of values to keep on stack)
    - lasti=True for entries where f_lasti needs preservation (finally-like)
    - lasti=False for simple except handlers

  ## Loop bytecode pattern (Python 3.13)

  FOR_ITER                 44 (to L4)   ← label, not raw offset in 3.13 display
  STORE_FAST               1 (item)
  [loop body]
  JUMP_BACKWARD           46 (to L1)
  END_FOR

  Note: "to L4" and "to L1" are logical labels (3.13+ display).
  JUMP_BACKWARD: backward relative jump, checks for interrupts.
  FOR_ITER's delta: jumps to END_FOR (or past loop) on exhaustion.

  ## Generator bytecode (Python 3.13)

  RETURN_GENERATOR          ← first instruction: creates generator, returns it
  RESUME                 0  ← actual entry point of generator body
  LOAD_CONST             2 (1)
  YIELD_VALUE            0  ← oparg=0 (not yield-from/await in 3.13)
  RESUME                 5  ← oparg=5 means AFTER_YIELD + GEN_EXPR_START bit?
                             Actually RESUME_AFTER_YIELD = 1, so oparg=5 unclear
                             [POSSIBLE: 5 = 4|1 = GEN_EXPR_START|AFTER_YIELD?]
  STORE_FAST             0 (x)

  Key pattern: RETURN_GENERATOR as first instruction, then RESUME 0.
  Generator body starts AFTER initial RETURN_GENERATOR.

  ## Specialization/adaptive opcodes (3.13)

  BINARY_OP may specialize to BINARY_OP_ADD_INT after runtime type observation.
  CACHE tracks hit count; when threshold reached, specialization applied.
  dis output with adaptive=True shows specialized opcodes.
  dis output with adaptive=False (default) shows base opcodes.
  For CFG construction: use default (non-adaptive) to see stable opcode names.

  ## match statement pattern

  MATCH_MAPPING, MATCH_KEYS, MATCH_SEQUENCE — for structural pattern matching.
  POP_JUMP_IF_FALSE used to skip non-matching cases.
  These add CFG branches in match arms.
<<<END>>>

